* Process states\nand threads
** States and transitions
*** New -> Ready (created, initialised)
*** Ready -> Running (elected by the scheduler)
*** Running -> Ready (preempted)
*** Running -> Waiting (I/O request, wait)
*** Waiting -> Ready (I/O done, resource available)
*** Running -> Zombie (terminated)
*** One Running process per CPU
*** Kernel keeps a list per state
** Thread
*** One execution context of a process
*** Every process has at least one (main)
*** Created from another thread
*** Own stack, own registers, own PC
*** Shares code, data, BSS, heap, files
*** Thread Control Block (TCB)
**** PC, registers, SP, state, priority
**** PCB points to its TCBs
*** Hyper-Threading: hardware threads
** POSIX threads
*** IEEE 1003.1c
*** Creation and termination
*** Synchronisation
*** Concurrent access
*** pthread_create(&tid, attr, start_routine, arg)
*** void *start_routine(void *)
*** pthread_join(tid, &retval)
*** pthread_exit(retval) or return
** Running threads
*** printHello example
*** Two threads incrementing count
*** Result varies: 200000, 149549, 123001
*** count++ is load, add, store
*** Data race, needs synchronisation
